// ===========================================================================
//  credentials.example.jsonc  ->  copy to  credentials.local.jsonc
// ===========================================================================
//
//  READ THIS FIRST
//
//  credentials.local.jsonc holds REAL PASSWORDS IN PLAINTEXT. It is
//  git-ignored (both this repo's .gitignore and demo-client/.gitignore name
//  it) and it is for local testing on your own machine only. It is not
//  encrypted, not a secret store, and not something to copy onto a server,
//  into a container, into a ticket, or into a screenshot.
//
//  THIS file - the example - is NOT git-ignored. Never put a real password
//  in it. Put them in credentials.local.jsonc.
//
//  It is read by ONE endpoint - GET /api/prefill/{service}/{provider} - which
//  exists only when the relay runs in Development and answers 404 anywhere
//  else. Its whole purpose is that testing a real provider does not mean
//  retyping a password twenty times. Nothing else reads it, and the relay
//  never sends it anywhere except back to your own browser tab.
//
//  Delete it when you are done. A file like this outliving its usefulness is
//  how a personal password ends up in a backup nobody remembers making.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  Shape:   { "<service>": { "<provider>": { "config": {…}, "inputs": {…} } } }
//
//  "config" and "inputs" are exactly the two halves of POST /login, and every
//  key below was GENERATED from the live manifests - auth.config and
//  auth.steps[].fields - not typed from memory. `curl localhost:8420/v1/providers`
//  prints those manifests, and the demo's connect form is rendered from the
//  same document.
//
//  "_comment" is ignored by the reader, and so are // comments like this one.
//  Unknown providers are ignored, so you can delete every entry you do not use.
//
//  DO NOT add a top-level KEY that is not a service name. The reader parses
//  the top level as a map of services, so an extra key there is a parse
//  failure that silently disables prefill for EVERY provider.
//
//  The mock-* providers need nothing here: they accept any input and touch no
//  network. Use them to exercise the flow without a real account.
// ===========================================================================
{
  "shop": {

    "bol": {
      "_comment": "bol.com. username is the e-mail you sign in with - the manifest states an e-mail pattern, so a member number or phone will be rejected by the form before it is ever sent. The connector signs in through a browser once and then reads the order history over bol's own GraphQL API. bol issues no refresh token, so the session is good for about a day and then wants a new sign-in; that is bol, not a bug.",
      "inputs": {
        "username": "",
        "password": ""
      }
    },

    "jumbo": {
      "_comment": "Jumbo. username is the e-mail you sign in to jumbo.com with. Jumbo sits behind a Cloudflare Turnstile bot check that cannot be answered anywhere but the rendering browser, so the connector types what it can and then hands you the live browser to finish. The session is a ~24h cookie with refreshable:false, so expect to sign in about once a day.",
      "inputs": {
        "username": "",
        "password": ""
      }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    //  ah  - nothing to put here.
    //
    //  Albert Heijn is auth.flow "remote_browser": its manifest declares NO
    //  config and NO input fields, because you sign in yourself in a browser
    //  the connector streams to your tab. There is no password for the relay
    //  to hold, which is the point. An "ah" entry here would be read and then
    //  ignored, so this file no longer pretends otherwise.
    //
    //  lidl - username and password again, as of 2026-08-03.
    //
    //  It briefly wanted a pasted callback URL. It no longer does: the
    //  sign-in happens in a browser we stream to you, so add it like the two
    //  above and include the config block, which the ticket URLs need:
    //
    //     "lidl": {
    //       "config": { "country": "NL", "language": "nl" },
    //       "inputs": { "username": "", "password": "" }
    //     }
    //
    //  Both inputs are OPTIONAL. Leave them out entirely and Lidl's own page
    //  is streamed to you from its first screen.
    //
    //  Both of the above were verified against the live manifests on
    //  2026-08-02 with `curl localhost:8420/v1/providers`. If a provider's
    //  flow changes again, this file is the thing that goes stale first -
    //  check it there rather than trusting these comments.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  }
}
